class.description:
Haxe uses `@:` metadata where GDScript uses `@` annotations. This page lists the metadata the engine interprets; any other `@:` metadata is left untouched for the Haxe compiler, which is what validates it.
Godot reads these annotations out of the source as it is parsed. A class annotated with `@:tool` runs in the editor, an exported variable appears in the Inspector, and a `@:signal` function declares a signal:
[codeblock lang=haxe]
package game;

import godot.Node2D;

@:tool
@:globalClass
class Player extends Node2D {
	@:exportGroup("Movement")
	@:exportRange(0, 500, 1) var speed:Float = 200.0;

	@:exportEnum("Idle,Walk,Run") var state:Int = 0;

	@:signal function state_changed(state:Int):Void {}
}
[/codeblock]
Every annotation has a shorter name and a `gd`-prefixed alias (`@:export`/`@:exported`, `@:signal`/`@:gdSignal`, `@:tool`/`@:gdTool`/`@:editor`, `@:globalClass`/`@:gdGlobalClass`). Both forms are accepted, so a project can pick the one that does not clash with a name already used by a Haxe library.
